; 32-bindop.kurt

; `bindop` marks a symbol as a *variable-binding operator*: its first
; argument is the variable (or a condition on it, e.g. `forall x>0 F(x)`) that
; is then considered bound inside the rest of the expression.  `forall` and `exists` (used already in lessons
; 19-let.kurt and 20-pick.kurt) are declared exactly this way, in `logic.kurt`,
; next to their axioms ("forall-elim" etc.):
;
;     arity  forall 2, exists 2
;     bindop forall, exists
;
; A symbol needs an `arity` (31-arity.kurt), or an `infix` (set.kurt's `|`),
; before it can be declared a `bindop`. The binding scope is fixed semantics, so `bindop` also makes the
; symbol a constant and prints that implied declaration. Here is a minimal binder of our own:

arity myforall 2
bindop myforall

bool P
const P
arity P 1

parse myforall x (P x)   ; (myforall x (P x)) -- `x` is bound in `(P x)`

; With a condition, the binder binds the first symbol of the condition, as
; written, that is new or a variable -- constants, predicates and the variables
; of enclosing binders are skipped. Kurt decides it when it reads the line and
; keeps it: the binder binds the same variable for the rest of the file (also
; when a block closes and its constants are free again; doc/kurt-doc.md §4.4).

const c
load numbers
use myforall (c < y) (P y)          ; binds `y`: `c` is a constant
use myforall (P z) (z = z)          ; binds `z`: `P` is a predicate
use myforall (0 < e) (myforall (e < w) (0 < w))   ; the inner one binds `w`: `e` is bound outside
expect "TypeError" "no variable to bind"
    use myforall (c < 1) (P c)      ; every symbol is a constant

; `bindop` sets up scope and checks that the first argument really is an
; unbound variable; the actual
; reasoning rules ("forall-elim" etc.) are ordinary `use` axioms, exactly
; like the ones in `logic.kurt`.

; The binding operators of the theories that come with Kurt, for ideas -- a
; binder is the right tool whenever a notation has a variable that only lives
; inside it:
;
;     sub x a T            minimal.kurt     T with a for x (substitution)
;     forall x P, ∀        logic.kurt       for all
;     exists x P, ∃        logic.kurt       there is
;     existsunique x P, ∃! logic.kurt       there is exactly one
;     { x ∈ A | P x }      set.kurt         the set-builder: `|` is an infix binder
;     sum i (a, b) T       analysis.kurt    T(a) + ... + T(b)
;     max x T, min x T     analysis.kurt    the largest/smallest value of T
;     argmax x T, argmin   analysis.kurt    where T is largest/smallest
;     sup x T, inf x T     analysis.kurt    the least upper/greatest lower bound
;     lim v a T            analysis.kurt    the limit of T for v towards a
;     tends v a L T        analysis.kurt    T tends to L for v towards a
;     cont x a T           continuity.kurt  T is continuous in x at a
;     diff x a T           continuity.kurt  T is differentiable in x at a
;     deriv x a T          continuity.kurt  the derivative of T in x at a
;     converges n L T      sequences.kurt   the sequence T converges to L
;     seqlim n T           sequences.kurt   the limit of the sequence T
;     series i a T         sequences.kurt   the series of T, from i = a
;     integral x (a, b) T  integral.kurt    the integral of T from a to b
;     integrable x (a, b) T  integral.kurt  T is integrable on [a, b]
;     λ x M                lambda.kurt      a function of x (lambda calculus)
;
; The arguments before the body that aren't the variable -- the point `a`,
; the range `(a, b)`, the limit `L` -- are outside the scope: `x` is bound
; only in the body (and in a condition, `∀ x > 0 ...`).
